  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "This is the hard version of the problem. The only difference between this version and the easy version is the constraints: here, the sum of NNN and the sum of MMM across all tests both won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105.\n\nThere are N+MN+MN+M points lying on the 2D coordinate plane. NNN of these points lie on the positive xxx-axis (i.e. are of the form (x,0)(x, 0)(x,0) for x&gt;0x \\gt 0x&gt;0), and the other MMM lie on the positive yyy-axis (i.e. are of the form (0,y)(0, y)(0,y) for y&gt;0y \\gt 0y&gt;0).\n\nIt is guaranteed that N≥3N \\ge 3N≥3 and M≥3M \\ge 3M≥3. Further, all N+MN+MN+M points are at distinct positions.\n\nYou must give each of these N+MN+MN+M points a color: either red or blue.\n\nThe only constraint is that each axis must have at least one point colored red and at least one point colored blue — aside from that, the coloring can be freely decided.\n\nDefine the monochromatic number of a coloring to be the **maximum area** of a **triangle** formed by some triple of points that all have the same color (either all three are red or all three are blue).\n\nYour task is find the minimum possible monochromatic number across all possible valid colorings of the points.\n\nIf the answer is AAA, you must output 2A2A2A.\n\nIt can be proved that under the constraints of this problem, 2A2A2A is always an integer.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of three lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM — the number of points on the xxx-axis and yyy-axis, respectively.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers x1,x2,…,xNx_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_Nx1​,x2​,…,xN​, meaning that the points on the xxx-axis are (x1,0),…,(xN,0)(x_1, 0), \\ldots, (x_N, 0)(x1​,0),…,(xN​,0).\nThe third line contains MMM space-separated integers y1,y2,…,yMy_1, y_2, \\ldots, y_My1​,y2​,…,yM​, meaning that the points on the yyy-axis are (0,y1),…,(0,yM)(0, y_1), \\ldots, (0, y_M)(0,y1​),…,(0,yM​).\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: the value 2A2A2A, where AAA is the minimum possible monochromatic area across all valid colorings.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤5⋅1041 \\leq T \\leq 5\\cdot 10^41≤T≤5⋅104",
      "3≤N,M≤2⋅1053 \\leq N, M \\leq 2\\cdot 10^53≤N,M≤2⋅105",
      "1≤xi,yi≤1091 \\le x_i, y_i \\le 10^91≤xi​,yi​≤109",
      "xi≠xjx_i \\ne x_jxi​=xj​ for i≠ji \\ne ji=j",
      "yi≠yjy_i \\ne y_jyi​=yj​ for i≠ji \\ne ji=j",
      "The sum of NNN and the sum of MMM over all test cases each won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3 3\n1 2 3\n2 1 3\n3 4\n7 3 9\n2 6 13 4\n4 4\n1 10 100 1000\n8 12 29 5",
        "output": "1\n21\n1188",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: One optimal solution is as follows:\n\nColor points (1,0),(0,2),(0,3)(1, 0), (0, 2), (0, 3)(1,0),(0,2),(0,3) red.\nThere is then only one triangle with all vertices red, namely the one with the three above vertices.\nIt can be verified that this triangle has an area of 0.50.50.5.\nColor points (2,0),(3,0),(0,1)(2, 0), (3, 0), (0, 1)(2,0),(3,0),(0,1) blue.\nAgain, there is then only one triangle with all vertices blue, and it can be verified to have an area of 0.50.50.5.\n\nThe maximum monochrome area of this configuration is hence max⁡(0.5,0.5)=0.5\\max(0.5, 0.5) = 0.5max(0.5,0.5)=0.5.\nIt can be proved that this is optimal.\nSince we are asked to output twice the optimal answer, we output 2⋅0.5=12\\cdot 0.5 = 12⋅0.5=1."
      }
    ]
  },
